  ],
  "text": "There is nothing mathematically special about ten. We just happen to have ten fingers. It feels like the natural way to count because it is the only one you have ever used. Twelve would arguably be better. It divides evenly by two, three, four and six. Ten only manages two and five. Try splitting ten into three fair parts. Other people chose differently, and you still live inside their choices. The Babylonians counted in sixty. Sixty seconds, sixty minutes, three hundred and sixty degrees. A four thousand year old fossil on your wrist. Twelve survives too. A dozen, twelve inches, twenty four hours. And some languages count in twenties, which is why French says four twenties for eighty. We did not pick the best base. We picked the one we were already holding. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
